# ON_HIT — he SWINGS it at something, and the wish happens to what he hit.
#
# The trigger a child asks for far more often than the tool could grant: "a sword that…", "a hammer
# that…", "gloves that…". Before this, `held` was the only thing close, and `held` means RIGHT-CLICK
# — so a sword wish came back as a wand. The item worked, it just was not the thing he asked for,
# and nothing in any gate could tell the difference.
#
# WHERE THE EFFECT HAPPENS. WishMeleeHit runs this AT what he hit and AS him, so both kinds of
# effect land correctly with no AIM line at all:
#
#   an effect that lands on the TARGET (hurt, ignite, freeze, launch) is already standing on it;
#   an effect that lands on HIM (heal, rich, speed, grow) addresses @s, which is still him.
#
# That is why this file has no AIM: the trigger already positioned everything. The held shape has to
# guess a reach because a right-click has no target; a swing has one by definition, which makes this
# the more accurate shape for any wish about hitting — and the one to prefer when he says "hits",
# "whack", "smack", "when I attack".
#
# (A placeholder written in a COMMENT is still a placeholder — the renderer scans the whole source.
# So the reach and the tags are spelled out in words here rather than named.)
import common

function give()
${GIVES}
    /tellraw @s {"text":"${GIVE_MESSAGE}","color":"${HEX}"}
end

function act()
    /function $namespace:effect
${YIELD}
end

${EFFECT_BLOCK}
